---
import translations from "@utils/t.ts";
import Layout from "@layouts/Layout.astro";
import Container from "@src/components/Container.astro";

const t = translations(Astro.url);
---

<Layout title="Workouts" description="My workout routine.">
	<Container class="pt-16">
		<div class="text-center">
			<h1 class="mb-4 text-3xl font-semibold">Workouts</h1>
		</div>
		<div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
			<div class="text-center">
				<p>
					<a target="_blank" href="https://github.com/Andrew-Chen-Wang/blog/commits/main/src/pages/workouts.astro">
						Edit History
					</a>
				</p>
			</div>
			<p set:html={t.workouts_description} />

			<p>
				In high school, I did some manual labor like roofing, house
				flipping, etc. that helped build endurance, so I thought I had
				decent enough muscles. I was also 115&ndash;120 lb, so I was
				pretty light and could do plenty of pull-ups and push-ups. I
				played soccer, and in my latter high school life I started to
				like endurance running since I was pretty bad at it before.
			</p>

			<p>
				Then college hit and I never did much exercise. I gained weight
				to 130 lb, and I noticed, wow, I had little muscle for my
				increasing weight. It was a girl I had liked who told me,
				&ldquo;Isn&rsquo;t it kind of strange how little you care about
				growing muscles? I thought that&rsquo;s what men were supposed
				to do to attract women.&rdquo; Whoops lol.
			</p>

			<p>These were my workout regiments inside my bedroom:</p>

			<h2>2022-01-10</h2>
			<p>Test day to see where I&rsquo;m at</p>
			<ul>
				<li>Plank: 1 set of 2.5 minutes</li>
				<li>Archer: 2 sets of 2 minutes</li>
				<li>
					Push-ups:
					<ul>
						<li>1 set of 20 regular</li>
						<li>10 elevated or burpees</li>
						<li>10 side by sides</li>
						<li>10 shoulder touches or explosive</li>
					</ul>
				</li>
				<li>Mountain hike: 2 sets of 30</li>
				<li>Bicep curls: 2 sets of 20 each arm, 10 lbs</li>
				<li>Crawling: 100 reps</li>
				<li>Oxygen apnea: 1 minute</li>
			</ul>
			<p>Incorporate:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>Side bicycle: 1 set of 20 each leg</li>
				<li>100 push-ups goal</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>2025-05-18</h2>
			<p>
				I&rsquo;ve been working out at the gym for the last 2 months
				like this: 3 sets / 10 reps per set =
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>30 seated military bench press</li>
				<li>30 bicep curls</li>
				<li>30x benching dumbbells</li>
				<li>
					30 weird bench lateral chest where your arms extend outwards
					and you bring it in above you
				</li>
				<li>20 dumbbell pullovers</li>
				<li>30 dumbbell front raises</li>
				<li>30 reverse fly</li>
				<li>30 push-ups</li>
				<li>
					1 minute hard wave, though apparently your back is supposed
					to be on the ground but mine hasn&rsquo;t
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The weird bench lateral chest, pullovers, front raises, and
				reverse fly have been using 25 lb; the rest are 30 lbs. Without
				protein powder, I was struggling a lot at home.
			</p>

			<p>
				In 2022, after about 1.5 months of consistently training every
				day, I think I increased my planks to 10 minutes, I have no idea
				what archer is anymore, I could do 100 push-ups consecutively, I
				was doing 100 mountain hikes, I stopped doing bicep curls
				because I was using a makeshift remedy using a drawstring bag
				and books, crawling 100 reps consistently, and I could do this
				free dive oxygen apnea for 3 minutes.
			</p>

			<p>
				Then I stopped and gained more weight. After working on startups
				and just life in general, I was still pretty skinny. I started
				dating someone, and her parents were like, this guy is gonna
				blow away in the wind. Flashback to the original quote, am I
				right?
			</p>

			<p>
				Once I left my last job, I realized how affordable the beauty
				premium was. Or at least, as an Asian male who&rsquo;d like to
				stand out &mdash; us Asian men are stereotyped as shy (for those
				who don&rsquo;t know me, I think sober drunk is a good
				descriptor of my personality, plus I don&rsquo;t drink) and
				scrawny. My sibling taught me that in our society (and
				personally I don&rsquo;t think stereotypes will ever go away),
				you can impress people easily by beating back stereotypes. For
				Asian men, that means looking less nerdy, appearing more
				confident, and in this case, looking fit. The thing that clicked
				for me was that I never had a tailored suit, so I would wear a
				suit, and a girl would say, &ldquo;You need this
				tailored,&rdquo; because I didn&rsquo;t fit in it; specifically,
				the arms being thin and my shoulders not being very broad. So in
				2025, I started working out, but with intention.
			</p>

			<p>
				I started March 2025 at a Planet Fitness, going 4 days a week,
				MTWFS. I was so weak I was lifting weights that my swimmer
				girlfriend could do initially. I started out at 15&ndash;17.5 lb
				for almost all of them except the front raises, which were lower
				at around 10 lb. Around September, I stopped working out as
				consistently, going maybe twice every two weeks because I burnt
				out on work, and the demotivation was clear. I had peaked at 40
				lb for each item; I think someone pointed out to me that it was
				strange I was doing hammer presses and dumbbell front raises at
				heavy weights with good form, and I think it had to do with the
				fact that Planet Fitness was so busy, I basically had to just
				use 1 set of dumbbells for the hour.
			</p>

			<p>
				By September, when I looked at myself in the mirror, I
				don&rsquo;t think I was necessarily a stick, but I&rsquo;m still
				skinny. There&rsquo;s some kind of psychology where they mention
				how one looks at themselves and rates their beauty more
				subjectively compared to what spectators would rate them. Maybe
				this is just my personality, but the mix of humbleness, possibly
				being blind, and the conflicting self-reflecting rating of
				myself (i.e., it&rsquo;s possible I never thought of myself as a
				stick, so now I still think I look the same just with a slightly
				bigger arm) &mdash; I&rsquo;ve been taking pictures and I barely
				notice any difference, but friends who hadn&rsquo;t seen me in 2
				years were saying, whoa, you&rsquo;ve definitely been working
				out arm, shoulder width, and chest wise. So I guess something
				happened.
			</p>

			<p>Here is my current workout regiment:</p>

			<h2>Day 1 &ndash; Push (Strength)</h2>
			<p>Chest &bull; Shoulders &bull; Triceps &bull; Core</p>
			<div class="overflow-x-auto">
				<table>
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th>Exercise</th>
							<th>Sets</th>
							<th>Current Weight</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td>Bench Press</td>
							<td>3&times;10</td>
							<td>50 lb</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Seated Overhead Press</td>
							<td>3&times;10</td>
							<td>50 lb</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Hammer Press</td>
							<td>3&times;10&ndash;12</td>
							<td>45 lb</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Push-ups</td>
							<td>2&times;25 (or to failure)</td>
							<td>Bodyweight</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Triceps Pushdowns</td>
							<td>3&times;12&ndash;15</td>
							<td>47.5 lb</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Lateral Raises</td>
							<td>4&times;15</td>
							<td>20 lb</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Hollow Hold</td>
							<td>
								2 &times; 1.5 min (work toward 2 min each)
							</td>
							<td>Bodyweight</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
			<p>Purpose: Heavy pressing and chest strength.</p>

			<h2>Day 2 &ndash; Pull (Strength)</h2>
			<p>Back &bull; Biceps &bull; Core</p>
			<div class="overflow-x-auto">
				<table>
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th>Exercise</th>
							<th>Sets</th>
							<th>Current Weight</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td>Row</td>
							<td>4&times;10</td>
							<td>90 lb</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Pull-ups (then Lat Pulldown if needed)</td>
							<td>4 sets</td>
							<td>90 lb pulldown</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Face Pulls</td>
							<td>4&times;15</td>
							<td>45.5 lb</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Rear Delt Fly</td>
							<td>3&times;15</td>
							<td>15 lb (Level 3)</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Bicep Curls</td>
							<td>3&times;10</td>
							<td>40&ndash;45 lb</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Weighted Plank</td>
							<td>3&times;45 sec</td>
							<td>25 lb</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
			<p>Purpose: Pulling strength, posture, shoulder health.</p>

			<h2>Day 3 &ndash; Push (Volume)</h2>
			<p>Chest &bull; Shoulders &bull; Triceps &bull; Core</p>
			<div class="overflow-x-auto">
				<table>
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th>Exercise</th>
							<th>Sets</th>
							<th>Current Weight</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td>Incline Bench</td>
							<td>3&times;12</td>
							<td>50 lb (3rd level)</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Arnold Press</td>
							<td>3&times;12</td>
							<td>30 lb</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Push-ups</td>
							<td>2&times;25</td>
							<td>Bodyweight</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Lateral Raises</td>
							<td>4&times;15</td>
							<td>20 lb</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Triceps Pushdowns or Dips</td>
							<td>3&times;12&ndash;15</td>
							<td>47.5 lb</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Cable Crunch</td>
							<td>4&times;12</td>
							<td>50 lb (Level 6)</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
			<p>
				Purpose: Upper chest, shoulder development, higher-volume
				pressing.
			</p>

			<h2>Day 4 &ndash; Pull (Volume)</h2>
			<p>Back &bull; Biceps &bull; Core</p>
			<div class="overflow-x-auto">
				<table>
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th>Exercise</th>
							<th>Sets</th>
							<th>Current Weight</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td>Chest-Supported Row</td>
							<td>3&times;12</td>
							<td>35 lb (3rd level)</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Lat Pulldown</td>
							<td>3&times;12</td>
							<td>90 lb</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Face Pulls</td>
							<td>3&times;15</td>
							<td>47.5 lb</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Rear Delt Fly</td>
							<td>3&times;15</td>
							<td>20 lb (Level 3)</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Bicep Curls (optional)</td>
							<td>3&times;12&ndash;15</td>
							<td>40 lb</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Hanging leg raises</td>
							<td>4&times;10&ndash;12</td>
							<td>Bodyweight</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Hollow Hold</td>
							<td>2 &times; 1.5 min</td>
							<td>Bodyweight</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
			<p>Purpose: Back thickness, posture, trunk stability.</p>

			<p>
				I properly started up again around the beginning of April, and I
				tested between 3 or 4 days. 4 days seem to fit me much better
				since 3 days gave me the feeling of too much rejuvenation.
			</p>

			<p>
				I will mention between September 2025 to March 2026, I basically
				somehow managed to stay relatively at the same weight class if I
				sometimes decided to stumble into the gym. It was kinda like
				maintenance. I recorded on 1/17/2026 a 35 lb dumbbell front
				raise and a 40 lb shoulder seated bench press. I&rsquo;m no
				longer increasing the weight of doing push exercises 5 lb at a
				time per month because I&rsquo;ve started doing pull exercises. When I
				first started, I just asked ChatGPT what exercises were good for
				chest and shoulder growth; I had decent shoulder traps already,
				so I just selected exercises that would help with target areas
				that I cared about. In retrospect, it shouldn&rsquo;t have
				mattered that much; as a beginner, according to a bunch of blogs
				online, doing any exercise consistently to simply gain strength
				is what matters, and there&rsquo;ll be a notable difference
				within a short time period.
			</p>

			<p>
				I stopped doing front raises and started doing lateral raises
				again. The front raises were starting to hurt my back right
				below my neck. I think in my journey, I feel rewarded for
				developing a habit for once. I feel like growing towards a goal
				that wasn&rsquo;t some big career goal was rewarding; I was not
				consistent in a lot of things growing up, which led to a
				personality of distractions. I loved bouncing between
				infotainment YouTube channels. I skipped medication. I sometimes
				skipped brushing my teeth. I stopped using my retainers (luckily
				only one tooth really moved, and it&rsquo;s the same tooth as my
				dad&rsquo;s, so it&rsquo;s just genetic :/). It feels good to
				have done this habit of working out for a good amount of time,
				that I&rsquo;ve started developing other small habits: actually
				waking up and cooking breakfast/lunch depending on when I sleep,
				sleeping on time, and developing a normal schedule/circadian
				rhythm around 11p&ndash;12a.
			</p>

			<p>
				When I first moved to NYC, I would inconsistently go out for
				runs. In sprints of around a month at a time, then just losing
				the habit, I would be consistent running 5 days a week, slowly
				growing from running about 2 miles without needing to stop to
				around 6.2 miles (almost a 10k) at a 7:30/mi pace to a 7 min/mi
				pace. It&rsquo;s been a year since I last tried running, and two
				years since I consistently have been.
			</p>

			<p>
				Now in my current workout, I think my weight increments are
				slowing down now that I&rsquo;m doing both push and pull instead
				of 4 push days. I'm starting to cut after peaking at 160lb. So now
				I&rsquo;m stacking even more on: I want
				to compete in a marathon one day. Since playing soccer, I&rsquo;ve
				always played defender, so it was a lot of sprinting rather than
				anything endurance wise. One of my best childhood friends
				mentioned my endurance was shit, so I took it to heart, and from
				junior year running at an 8 min/mi pace for 1 mile (I stopped
				exercising during the school year outside of high school soccer;
				in freshman year, I could achieve a 6 minute/mi pace), I started
				running longer and longer (inconsistently; habits is just hard for me). I had an
				internal injury unfortunately by senior year as I was gunning
				for a marathon, so I had to stop. At that time, I was running 1
				mile uphill at a 6:30/mi pace and going at least 4 miles per
				day. I think I can beat that consistency and mindset back into
				me. I think building habits has been vital in building
				confidence in myself to do a bunch of things. I'm just much heavier now.
			</p>

			<p>
				If you read my goals blog, I mention I&rsquo;ve always struggled
				with marketing. There is a public perception anxiety that runs
				in my family, particularly me and my mom. But with habits, I
				think I can overcome the anxiety and do better.
			</p>

			<p>
				And now same goes for marathon training. Same goes for
				marketing. Unlike coding, which back then I had a love and
				passion for, these things aren&rsquo;t necessarily a love or
				passion but more so just a thing I like and is good for me. At
				least I like it. But it wasn&rsquo;t something that drew me,
				just more like &ldquo;I should probably do this&rdquo; without
				the &ldquo;ugh&rdquo; feeling in the beginning for many dreaders
				of exercising. I think I don&rsquo;t really dread it since
				I&rsquo;ve been cardio wise pretty fit and skinny my whole life.
				I&rsquo;ll write a blog on what I think &ldquo;interest&rdquo;
				means in people, but it boils down to what are you good at.
				I&rsquo;m not the best at anything &mdash; maybe I&rsquo;m
				highly proficient at pattern matching, thus conversationally I
				make a lot of analogies &mdash; but this is probably why
				I&rsquo;m constantly drawn to exercise.
			</p>

			<p>
				That&rsquo;s my current workout regiment, why I do it, how I
				feel, my goals, and how much longer I can continue. This is a living
				document, so I'll cut down on words, re-organize sections to be a more
				coherent story, and update this page with current weight numbers to
				have a record of progress and show people how long it takes to grow
				(with probably not enough protein).
			</p>
		</div>
	</Container>
</Layout>
